    "result_text": "The full test suite is green: **279 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented telephone normalization so that numbers are stored digits-only and duplicates are detected regardless of punctuation.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Relaxed the `telephone` validation pattern from `^[0-9]*$` to `^[-0-9 ()]*$` so that punctuated input like `(613) 555-0100` passes DTO bean-validation instead of being rejected with a 400. (Length stays capped at 20, minLength 1.)\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added a `normalizeTelephone(...)` helper that strips spaces, dashes and parentheses, leaving digits only. It is applied:\n   - In `addOwner`, right after mapping the DTO \u2014 so the value is normalized *before* the uniqueness check and *before* persisting. This is what makes `(613) 555-0100` and `6135550100` collide as duplicates, and stores the canonical digit-only form.\n   - In the `isDuplicateOwner` comparison (both sides normalized).\n   - In `updateOwner`, so edits are stored in the same canonical form.\n\nThe `Owner` entity's existing `^[0-9]{10}$` constraint remains satisfied because the value is already stripped to 10 digits before it reaches the entity/persistence layer.\n\nThe new `Cp11Tests` (`coreStoresDigitsOnly` and `functionalityNormalisedDuplicateRejected`) both pass, confirming the stored value is returned digits-only in the `telephone` field and that a punctuated duplicate is rejected with 409 Conflict.",
